<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanara</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Canara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kanara, or Canara, is the coastal region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. Its ports, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalore">Mangalore</a>, Bhatkal and Honnavar, traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and other goods with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and later the Portuguese. Under British rule it was divided into the districts of North Kanara and South Kanara.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanara.jpg" alt="Kanara" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raghu Naik NC · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kanara, called Karāvali by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> speakers after their word for coast, covers about 10,000 square kilometres on the south-western coast of Peninsular India and forms the northern segment of the Malabar coast. It is bounded by Konkan to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> to the east, the Kerala Plains to the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> to the west, and stretches about 225 kilometres from north to south, with a maximum width of about 64 kilometres in the south.</p><p>According to the historian Severino da Silva, the ancient name of the region is Parashurama Srushti, the creation of Parashurama. He and Stephen Fuchs hold that the name Canara was invented by the Portuguese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English who came to trade from the early sixteenth century: they knew the ruling Bednore Dynasty as the Kannada Dynasty and pronounced the d as an r. They add that the question is controversial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Since antiquity much of the coast has belonged to the culturally distinct area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulu-nadu">Tulu Nadu</a>, which historically lay between the Gangavalli River in the north and the Chandragiri River in the south; the land north of the Gangavalli is traditionally counted as part of the Konkan. In 1799, after the Fourth Mysore War, the British took the region and made it the Canara district of the Madras Presidency. The district was split in 1859: North Canara went to the Bombay Presidency, while South Canara, which included Kasaragod, stayed with Madras.</p><p>After independence in 1947 the Bombay Presidency became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>. Under the States Reorganisation Act of 1956 the southern part of Bombay State joined Mysore State, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> in 1972, and Kasaragod went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. North and South Canara were later renamed Uttara Kannada and Dakshina Kannada, and in 1997 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udupi">Udupi</a> district was carved out of Dakshina Kannada.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kanara comprises the districts of Uttara Kannada, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udupi">Udupi</a> and Dakshina Kannada, with their capitals at Karwar, Udupi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalore">Mangalore</a>, and extends from the village of Majali in the north to Talapady in the south. The three districts had a combined population of 4,704,179 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Census</a>. Kannada, Konkani and Tulu are the languages most closely associated with the region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kanara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alupa kings issued distinctive gold and copper coins and ruled through local chiefs, profiting from the trade of the <b>Kanara</b> ports with Arabia and the Persian Gulf.</blockquote><a href="/state/alupa-dynasty">Alupa dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Shivappa Nayaka, the greatest ruler, introduced a celebrated land revenue system, the shist, and expanded the kingdom to the coast of <b>Kanara</b>, driving the Portuguese from several ports.</blockquote><a href="/state/keladi-nayaka-kingdom">Keladi Nayaka kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
